  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This envelope variant is a usable substrate for carrying feed or CAS bytes, and its deterministic CBOR plus explicit pCID dispatch give clear layer boundaries, but it intentionally does not define CAS verification policy, peer-local accounting, retry logic, or signatures. For a corrupt-chunk replication scenario, that makes it only a weak partial fit.",
    "strengths": [
      "Deterministic CBOR bytes support stable hashing of the exact envelope bytes.",
      "The spec clearly pushes content-addressed references and feed semantics into the payload protocol instead of hidden global machinery.",
      "Unknown pCID handling is explicit and avoids speculative parsing or central registry dependence."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The variant has no built-in signature or authorship evidence, limiting blame and audit trails after corruption.",
      "It does not specify local rejection, accounting, quarantine, or retry behavior for hash-mismatching chunks.",
      "Opaque payload handling leaves the key scenario behavior to another protocol not defined here."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "If this specimen is treated as a complete replication answer, corrupt data may be detectable by hash yet still poorly attributable or rate-limited.",
      "Unsigned envelopes weaken peer-local accountability in adversarial replication paths."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which payload or transport protocol records the local accounting entry when a chunk fails CID verification?",
      "Is CID C expected to name the outer envelope bytes, the payload bytes, or both?",
      "Should unsigned-v0 envelopes be allowed on replication paths without an authenticated wrapper?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
